The reporter agent is still working through its remaining summaries. I haven't seen it write anything yet, so I'll wait for it to finish.

Another summary arrived, but the 10-19 report still isn't written. `reports/agentic-coding/` still ends at the 10-12 report.

This one looks like the lead story. On 2026-10-13, Anthropic published "Redesigning source control at Anthropic" ([source](https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/redesigning-source-control)).

- **What they built:** They replaced git for their monorepo with an in-house system called Scion. They also built a code review tool called Antique, plus code search and source-control IAM.
- **Why:** Merges per day grew from about 2,500 in May to over 30,000 by September. Git's distributed, offline assumptions fit poorly with clients that are mostly always-online agents and jobs. Scaling it meant running several hundred internal git mirrors.
- **Scale:** Main-branch history is almost 100 GB, and there are about 25 million checkouts a day for CI alone.
- **Results they report:**
  - About 200x less CPU and memory than the old mirroring setup.
  - Checkouts take seconds instead of minutes.
  - CI CPU capacity is up about 25%.
  - Cache hit rate is over 99% per tier.
- **Effort:** Seven people built it over five months.

For a platform org, the takeaway is that agent-driven merge volume can break your source-control layer before it breaks anything else. These figures are Anthropic's own and not independently checked.

The reporter agent is still working through its remaining summaries. I'll wait for it to finish.
